RDG DIVISION CALCULUS version 1.1b
version 1.1
closed typed signature over the locked 1.0-base

STATUS
RDG is not a field.
Division is not arithmetic. It is relational inversion:
structure composed with inverted evaluation.
Results are geometries, not numbers.
a/0o_E is a mode shift, not a contradiction of field axioms.
Three levels stay distinct:
    common pre-quotient generator U
    RDG quotient presentation
    MFE evaluative presentation
T_RDG and D_MFE are presentations of one substrate, not two primitives.


1. SUBSTRATE

Primitive chain:
    point ---> counterpoint ---> couple (bipolar coupling)

Primitives of U, all four:
    p            point
    p*           counterpoint of p
    C(p, p*)     forward couple
    C(p*, p)     return couple

Primitive incidences:
    p -> p*
    p* -> C(p, p*)
    C(p, p*) -> C(p*, p)
    C(p*, p) -> p

U is exactly these primitives plus these incidences.
loop(p) is not a primitive of U.
Gen_PC is the generator of the spine.
Im(Gen_PC) is the set of geometries with a provenance certificate.


2. RDG QUOTIENT

~_RDG partitions all of U. q_RDG is total.

    C(p*, p) ~_RDG p
    C(p, p*) ~_RDG p*
    p not~_RDG p*

Classes:
    [p]_RDG  = { p, C(p*, p) }
    [p*]_RDG = { p*, C(p, p*) }

Forward couple descends to the counterpoint class.
Return couple descends to the point class.
An incidence whose endpoints are identified under ~_RDG
is a quotient loop, not a new point.
loop(p) denotes that quotient-incidence presentation.
It is not an element of U and not a third class.

    q_RDG : U -> U / ~_RDG
    T_RDG = presentation of U / ~_RDG with quotient loops drawn

The primitive quotient has two classes.
The 3-count is a presentation count of classes plus induced loops,
not the number of primitive classes.


3. MFE QUOTIENT

On the basic coupling spine U, ~_MFE is identity:
    x ~_MFE y iff x = y

q_MFE restricted to U is an isomorphism of that spine.
D_MFE is the evaluative, mode-resolved presentation of the
retained coupling data, not a point-collapsing quotient.
The four-point dyad is that presentation of the four primitives.

~_MFE remains available for nontrivial identifications only on
extended configurations, derived incidences, and higher-order
evaluative patterns. No such identification is in the base signature.

    q_MFE : U -> U / ~_MFE
    D_MFE = evaluative presentation of U / ~_MFE
    alpha in {RDG, MFE}
    q_alpha : U -> U / ~_alpha


4. FIBERS

    SID_RDG  structural presentation of U / ~_RDG
    PED_MFE  evaluative presentation of U / ~_MFE

SID runs along q_RDG.
PED runs along q_MFE.
This split is a restriction, not a naming convenience.
They meet in U via certificates (U, alpha).
They are not one undifferentiated presentation.
An MFE term becomes SID-visible only as the composite
    SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
which is not itself a quotient presentation of U.
The sort SID contains the RDG image plus uncertified composites.
SID_RDG names the image, not every SID term.


5. SORTS AND MODES

Sorts:
    Obj     raw objects
    SID     structural terms
    PED     evaluative geometries
    INV     inverted evaluative geometries
    EXP     expansion sub-sort of SID
    COL     collapse sub-sort of SID
    BOT     clash sort, outside SID

REL is not in the base signature.
BOT is a sort. bot is the constant of that sort.

    bot : BOT

Sort/map split:
    INV is the sort.
    iota : PED -> INV is the map.
    PED^{-1}(b) means iota(PED(b)), not a second map.

Modes:
    O object
    S structure
    E evaluation
    I inverted evaluation

Inclusions:
    EXP subset SID
    COL subset SID
    EXP intersect COL = empty
    EXP intersect BOT = empty
    COL intersect BOT = empty
    BOT not subset SID

EXP(X) != bot is closed under every future extension.
A constructor that identifies them is a different calculus.

Marks:
    cross-mode maps carry _in^out
    in-mode maps carry a single mode index
    mark erasure is notation only and does not change the mode


6. OBJECTS AND ZERO GEOMETRIES

    0o_S : Obj     no structural degrees of freedom
    0o_E : Obj     empty evaluation support

Bare 0 is not a term.
Numerator zero is 0o_S.
Denominator zero is 0o_E.

    Z_S := SID(0o_S) : SID
    Z_E := PED(0o_E) : PED
    Z_I := iota(Z_E) : INV


7. INV MONOID AND Z_I

    circ_I : INV x INV -> INV
    id_I : INV
    J circ_I id_I = J = id_I circ_I J
    circ_I is associative

    Z_I != id_I
    Z_I is a designated invertible nonidentity INV element
    exists Z_I^{-1} : INV
        Z_I^{-1} circ_I Z_I = id_I
        Z_I circ_I Z_I^{-1} = id_I

Factoring, retained:
    for every J : INV there exists a unique K : INV
        J = K circ_I Z_I
    equivalently
        K = J circ_I Z_I^{-1}
    right-composition by Z_I is a bijection on INV

Z_I is not a terminal object.
Z_I is not a greatest element.
No order <= on INV is declared.
Z_I is not a global absorber of evaluative constraint.
    iota(PED(b)) circ_I Z_I = Z_I is not a law.
Z_I is not a unit for division.
Z_I is the reversible INV phase used in cross-mode expansion.
Z_S circ_{S,I} Z_I = bot is a cross-mode exception.
It does not make Z_I null or identity-like inside INV.


8. GENERATORS

    SID : Obj -> SID_RDG
        SID(a) via U -> U / ~_RDG
        structured trace of point -> counterpoint -> couple

    PED : Obj -> PED_MFE
        PED(b) via U -> U / ~_MFE

    iota : PED -> INV
        isomorphism
        mode inversion, not a numeric inverse, not a ring inverse
        flips evaluation direction, not magnitude
        not declared on SID
        not declared on Obj

    inv_2 : INV -> PED
        total inverse of iota
        inv_2(iota(E)) = E
        iota(inv_2(J)) = J
        for every E : PED and every J : INV

    ev : SID -> PED
        total on SID
        iota(ev(X)) is the SID-to-iota bridge
        totality of ev does not certify the result
        quotient-sensitive reductions still require a certificate

Certificate-level maps, not object generators:
    SID_U : U -> SID
    SID_q : U / ~_alpha -> SID
    q_alpha^SID : SID -> SID
    used only inside a present certificate (U, alpha)

Rejected without a certificate:
    treating SID(SID(a)) as a quotient lift
Rejected outright:
    iota(SID(a))
    iota(Y · Z) without ev
    SID(iota(E))


9. WITNESS AND EQUIVALENCE

(U, alpha) is a side certificate, not part of the syntactic term.
A certified term is the term plus the certificate
    "this term presents q_alpha(U)".
Provenance-gated lifts and reductions require the certificate.
A term with no certificate is unproven and cannot use those reductions.

~= is isomorphism of quotient geometries of U under alpha.
Not term identity. Not mere mode relabeling.
~= applies only to terms that carry an admissible certificate.
~= does not identify uncertified SID composites.

~= may identify quotient representatives, point labels inherited
from U, and mode labels that do not change the quotient incidence.
~= does not identify RDG with MFE unless both arise from the same
quotient of U under the same alpha.

Coherence:
    if (U1, alpha) and (U2, alpha) both certify the same term,
    then q_alpha^SID(SID_U(U1)) ~= q_alpha^SID(SID_U(U2))

Square, only when the certificate is present:

    SID_q . q_alpha ~= q_alpha^SID . SID_U


10. COMPOSITION

Units, in-mode only:
    id_S : SID
    id_E : PED
    id_I : INV
    X circ_mu id_mu = X = id_mu circ_mu X
    for mu in {S, E, I}

In-mode, associative and unital:
    circ_S : SID x SID -> SID
    circ_E : PED x PED -> PED
    circ_I : INV x INV -> INV
    (X circ_mu Y) circ_mu Z = X circ_mu (Y circ_mu Z)

Cross-mode:
    circ_{S,I} : SID x INV -> SID + BOT
    normal result: SID
    designated exception:
        Z_S circ_{S,I} Z_I = bot
    every other (S, I) pair lands in SID

    circ_{S,E} : SID x PED -> BOT
    circ_{E,S} : PED x SID -> BOT
    circ_{I,S} : INV x SID -> BOT
    circ_{E,I} : PED x INV -> BOT unless a descent is declared
    circ_{I,E} : INV x PED -> BOT unless a descent is declared

Defined rather than bot iff the pair is (S,S), (E,E), (I,I),
or (S,I) other than the designated exception.

Cross-mode unit is not a law:
    X circ_{S,I} id_I = X is not declared
So EXP(X) = X is not forced.


11. DIVISION

    /   : Obj x Obj -> SID + BOT
    /_g : SID x PED -> SID + BOT

    a / b := SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
    X /_g E := X circ_{S,I} iota(E)

Result of a successful composite: SID, mark _S^S.
Division is one application of circ_{S,I}, not a free fraction bar.
Numerator contributes structure.
Denominator contributes evaluation, inverted.
Composition yields a geometry, not a number.
Total into SID + BOT. Partial as a map into SID.
Only SID-failure is the zero-zero pair.

    a / b = SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
    a / 0o_E = EXP(SID(a)) = SID(a) circ_{S,I} Z_I
    0o_S / a = COL(iota(PED(a))) = Z_S circ_{S,I} iota(PED(a))
    0o_S / 0o_E = Z_S circ_{S,I} Z_I = bot

    EXP : SID -> EXP
    EXP(X) := X circ_{S,I} Z_I
    COL : INV -> COL
    COL(J) := Z_S circ_{S,I} J

    a / 0o_E = c / 0o_E  iff  SID(a) = SID(c)
    0o_S / a = 0o_S / c  iff  iota(PED(a)) = iota(PED(c))
    EXP(X) = EXP(Y) iff X = Y
    COL(J) = COL(K) iff J = K
    EXP(X) != bot
    COL(J) != bot

Z_I retains structural degrees of freedom of the left factor
in the cross-mode reading.
It does not absorb inside INV.


12. NESTED DIVISION

circ_{S,I} returns SID + BOT, so repetition is a lifted left action,
not a second use of the unlifted operator.

    /_g^L : (SID + BOT) x PED -> SID + BOT

    X /_g^L E := X circ_{S,I} iota(E)    when X : SID
    bot /_g^L E := bot                   when E : PED

    (a / b) / c := (a / b) /_g^L PED(c)

If a / b inhabits SID:
    (a / b) / c
      = (SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(c))

If a / b = bot:
    (a / b) / c = bot

Not admitted:
    SID(a) circ_{S,I} (iota(PED(b)) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(c)))
because circ_{S,I} expects SID on the left and INV on the right.

Only the left association is legal.
No outer SID on a geometry.


13. INVERSE

    b^{-1} := iota(PED(b))     when b : Obj
    E^{-1} := iota(E)          when E : PED
    inv_2(iota(E)) = E
    iota(inv_2(J)) = J

Not a ring inverse. Not multiplicative by default.

b/b is permanently not a unit.
    b / b := SID(b) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
is a cross-mode term, not id_S, and not neutral for circ_S.
No normalization quotient identifies b/b with id_S.

Optional squares, not assumed:
    iota(E1 oplus E2) = iota(E1) oplus_I iota(E2)
        only if oplus is parallel and channelwise
    iota(E1 otimes E2) = iota(E2) otimes_I iota(E1)
        only if otimes is declared and iota is converse-like
If a square is not declared, that PED operation does not descend to INV.


14. PED OPERATIONS

    oplus  : PED x PED -> PED
        parallel evaluation channels
    otimes : PED x PED -> PED
        sequential or nested evaluation

oplus and otimes do not participate in the definition of division.
They do not constrain circ_{S,I}.
No equation relates them to / unless a commutation square is declared.
No distributivity, compatibility, lifting, or reduction connects them to /.


15. MULTIPLICATION

    · : SID x SID -> SID
    a · b := SID(a) circ_S SID(b)
    X · Y := X circ_S Y
    (X · Y) · Z = X · (Y · Z)
    X · id_S = X = id_S · X

Associative and unital inside SID mode only.
id_S is a geometry unit, not an object unit.
No object-level product mul_O is declared.
a / (b · c) is not a term, because b · c : SID and / expects Obj.

Geometry-level product denominator:
    E_bc := ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c)) : PED
    a /_g E_bc
      := SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c)))

Well-typed product bridge:
    iota(ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c)))
Rejected:
    iota(SID(b) circ_S SID(c))


16. REDUCTION

No general identity:
    (a / b) / c != a /_g ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c))

Left-associated nested form, on the SID branch:
    (SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(c))

Product form:
    SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c)))

Rewrite
    (a / b) / c ---> a /_g ev(SID(b) circ_S SID(c))
only when both hold:
    1. certificate (U, alpha) is attached, the nested composite
       presents q_alpha(U), and
         SID_q(q_alpha(U)) ~= q_alpha^SID(SID_U(U))
    2. iota converse is declared on the extracted evaluation
         iota(ev(X circ_S Y)) = iota(ev(Y)) circ_I iota(ev(X))

ev being total does not supply the certificate.
Uncertified terms cannot use the rewrite.
If a / b = bot, the nested term is bot and the rewrite does not fire.
Otherwise the two forms stay distinct geometries.
Design-level equivalence, not a law.


17. BOTTOM

    bot := Z_S circ_{S,I} Z_I
    also produced by every table row whose result is BOT

Base propagation, the only bottom rule required for nesting:
    bot /_g^L E = bot

No REL carrier.
No untyped BOT circ R.
Absorption does not rewrite EXP(X) or COL(J) to bot
when bot is not an argument.
bot is not EXP. bot is not COL.
Geometric clash constant, not a numeric bottom.


18. CONSTRUCTORS

    F_S : SID -> SID
    F_E : PED -> PED
    F_I : INV -> INV

Gate, declared or absent:
    F_I(iota(E)) = iota(F_E(E))    for every E : PED

When the gate is declared and the composite is in SID, not bot:
    F_S(SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b)))
      ---> F_S(SID(a)) circ_{S,I} iota(F_E(PED(b)))

In /_g notation, same rule:
    F_S(a / b) ---> F_S(SID(a)) /_g F_E(PED(b))
    only on the SID branch

F_S . iota is ill-typed and is not the gate.
Absent the declaration, division stays external.
This blocks F(a / b) => F(a) / F(b) as a default.


19. EQUALITY

Congruence:
    X = X' and Y = Y' => X circ_mu Y = X' circ_mu Y'
        when both composites are formed in the same sort
    E = E' => iota(E) = iota(E')
    J = J' => inv_2(J) = inv_2(J')
    a = a' => SID(a) = SID(a')
    b = b' => PED(b) = PED(b')

Definitional equations are only those stated in this specification.
(a / b) / c is not identified with the product form unless section 16 fires.
Branch equations are conditional on the SID summand.


20. ADDITION, RESERVED

Not in the base signature.
If + is introduced:
    + : SID x SID -> SID
    in-mode only
    must not reopen / or the provenance gate
Z_S is not required to be the unit of +.
The unit of + must be declared explicitly.
Numerator additivity is forbidden by default.
    (a + c) / b
requires a new rule connecting + with SID. No such rule exists.
Without that declaration, + does not interact with /, circ_{S,I}, or iota.


21. STABLE SIGNATURE

    SID   : Obj -> SID_RDG
    PED   : Obj -> PED_MFE
    iota  : PED -> INV          isomorphism
    inv_2 : INV -> PED          inverse of iota
    ev    : SID -> PED          total, not a certificate
    SID_U : U -> SID            certificate-level
    SID_q : U / ~_alpha -> SID  certificate-level
    circ_S     : SID x SID -> SID
    circ_E     : PED x PED -> PED
    circ_I     : INV x INV -> INV
    circ_{S,I} : SID x INV -> SID + BOT
    ·     : SID x SID -> SID
    /     : Obj x Obj -> SID + BOT
    /_g   : SID x PED -> SID + BOT
    /_g^L : (SID + BOT) x PED -> SID + BOT
    EXP   : SID -> EXP
    COL   : INV -> COL
    bot   : BOT
    q_RDG : U -> U / ~_RDG
    q_MFE : U -> U / ~_MFE

Defining equations:
    a / b   := SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
    X /_g E := X circ_{S,I} iota(E)
    X /_g^L E := X circ_{S,I} iota(E)    if X : SID
    bot /_g^L E := bot
    (a / b) / c := (a / b) /_g^L PED(c)
    a · b   := SID(a) circ_S SID(b)
    b^{-1}  := iota(PED(b))
    E^{-1}  := iota(E)
    Z_S     := SID(0o_S)
    Z_E     := PED(0o_E)
    Z_I     := iota(Z_E)
    Z_I != id_I
    Z_I^{-1} circ_I Z_I = id_I = Z_I circ_I Z_I^{-1}
    J = (J circ_I Z_I^{-1}) circ_I Z_I
    EXP(X)  := X circ_{S,I} Z_I
    COL(J)  := Z_S circ_{S,I} J
    a / 0o_E   := EXP(SID(a))
    0o_S / a   := COL(iota(PED(a)))
    0o_S / 0o_E := bot
    [p]_RDG  = { p, C(p*, p) }
    [p*]_RDG = { p*, C(p, p*) }
    x ~_MFE y iff x = y    on the basic spine U


22. VARIABLES

Objects: a, b, c, p, p*, 0o_S, 0o_E
Couples: C(p, p*), C(p*, p)
Classes: [p]_RDG, [p*]_RDG
Presentation mark: loop(p), not a primitive
Substrate: U, Gen_PC, Im(Gen_PC)
Quotients: q_RDG, q_MFE, q_alpha, ~_RDG, ~_MFE, ~_alpha
Traces: SID, PED, SID_U, SID_q, q_alpha^SID
Inversion: iota, inv_2, Z_I^{-1}, b^{-1}, E^{-1}
Bridge: ev
Composition: circ_S, circ_E, circ_I, circ_{S,I}
Units: id_S, id_E, id_I
Operations: /, /_g, /_g^L, ·, oplus, otimes
Singular: EXP, COL, BOT, bot, Z_S, Z_E, Z_I
Constructors: F_S, F_E, F_I
Geometries: X, Y, Z, E, J, K
Modes: O, S, E, I
Sorts: Obj, SID, PED, INV, EXP, COL, BOT
Fibers: SID_RDG, PED_MFE
Presentations: T_RDG, D_MFE


23. LOCKED REFUSALS

No field identity unless the certified rewrite fires.
No default distributivity of iota.
No default commutation of SID with iota.
No SID-on-SID quotient lift without a side certificate.
No iota on SID terms.
No right-associated nested division.
No b/b = id_S.
No numerator additivity by default.
No Z_S as an automatic unit of +.
No EXP(X) = bot, in this signature or any extension of it.
No single sort containing both the trifoil and the dyad as presentations.
No Z_I = id_I.
No reading of Z_I as terminal, lattice top, or global absorber.
No loop(p) as a primitive or as a third RDG class.
No partial ~_RDG: C(p*, p) is classed with p.
No nontrivial base identifications in ~_MFE.
No ~= on uncertified composites.
No cross-mode unit law.
No bare 0.
No REL in the base signature.
No untyped BOT circ R.
No object-level product in the base signature.
oplus and otimes have no equation against / until a square is declared.
ev total does not certify a term.

24. APPENDIX Q
collapse and recovery via the Q-box
derived, not a primitive of the base signature
version 1.1 is unchanged if this appendix is absent

STATUS
RDG does not require a bolted-on homomorphism
    collapse : SID x PED -> R
as a primitive of division.
The collapse is the Q-box, the slice-moderation operator
that unifies a structural channel and an evaluative channel
into one evaluative slice.
Q is not circ_{S,I}.
Q is not ev.
Q is not q_alpha.
Q does not rewrite a / b.
Q does not identify SID_RDG with PED_MFE inside the base.
RDG is not a conservative extension of a ring.
Removing this appendix leaves 1.1 intact.

A. ROLE

The Q-box:
    1. forgets the SID/PED mode separation in its output
    2. projects a relational configuration into one evaluative slice
    3. recovers scalar-like behavior on the total fragment
    4. implements the collapse of relational geometry into a
       field-compatible domain

Formal action:
    Q tensor SID.S  --->  PED.D

    SID.S    structural channel, terms X : SID
    PED.D    dynamic evaluative slice, the output sort of Q
    PED.D is not a second name for PED_MFE
    PED.D is not a quotient of U

ev : SID -> PED stays in PED_MFE and does not certify.
circ_{S,I} : SID x INV -> SID + BOT stays in that coproduct.
Those two maps are not the collapse.

B. DERIVED COLLAPSE

    collapse(X, E) := Q(X, E)
    X : SID
    E : PED
    Q : SID x PED -> PED.D + BOT

Not a primitive of the base signature.
Derived by this appendix.
The recovery map is this operator, not an extra generator of division.

Reading of a division term, supplied after the base equation:
    a / b = SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
    remains the base equation.
    Q is applied to the pair (SID(a), PED(b)), not to the
    composite as a quotient of U.

C. CLAUSES

Total fragment, neither factor zero:
    Q(X, E) = s(X, E) : PED.D
    when X is not Z_S and E is not Z_E
    s is the slice interpretation

Expansion, denominator zero:
    Q(X, Z_E) = s_exp(X) : PED.D
    corresponding to EXP(X) = X circ_{S,I} Z_I
    s_exp is the scalar-limit reading of expansion
    if s_exp is not declared, Q(X, Z_E) is undefined, not bot

Collapse, numerator zero:
    Q(Z_S, E) = s_col(E) : PED.D
    corresponding to COL(iota(E)) = Z_S circ_{S,I} iota(E)
    s_col is the scalar-limit reading of collapse
    if s_col is not declared, Q(Z_S, E) is undefined, not bot

Clash:
    Q(Z_S, Z_E) = bot
    matching 0o_S / 0o_E = bot
    the only clause that returns bot

bot has no Q image.
    Q is not defined on BOT.
    there is no Q(bot).

D. RELATION TO ORDINARY ARITHMETIC

When Q is applied:
    the multi-sorted geometry is read as one evaluative slice
    division on the total fragment is read as ordinary division
        in that slice
    EXP and COL are read as scalar limits when s_exp and s_col
        are declared, and as undefined when they are not
    0o_S / 0o_E is read as bot, not as a scalar
    SID/INV relational information is lost in the output

This loss is the collapse. It is not a base rewrite.
EXP(X) != bot and COL(J) != bot remain refusals of version 1.1.
An appendix that sets s_exp or s_col equal to bot is a different appendix.

No ring homomorphism is claimed beyond the declared clauses.
No map PED.D -> R is claimed unless a further reading of the
slice is declared. Scalar-like means PED.D, not automatically R.
obs : PED.D -> R, if added, is that further reading and is
not implied by Q.

E. PRESERVED AND ERASED

If Q(X, E) and Q(X', E') both inhabit PED.D, then
    X = X' and E = E' implies Q(X, E) = Q(X', E').
Q does not have to preserve circ_S, circ_E, or circ_{S,I}.
Any such preservation is a separate declaration.
Q forgets the RDG/MFE presentation split in its output only.
The certificate (U, alpha) is not implied by Q, and Q is not
implied by the certificate.
Q is not the gate for (a / b) / c ---> product form.

Off the total fragment the 1.1 locks remain in force:
    b / b != id_S
    no numerator additivity
    EXP(X) != bot
    COL(J) != bot
    Z_I != id_I
    no cross-mode unit
    ~= certified only

F. DESIGN

RDG's legitimacy does not depend on being arithmetic with a
new division. It depends on:
    internal consistency of the multi-sorted signature
    the quotient split, two RDG classes and identity-on-spine MFE
    typed cross-mode composition
    explicit singularity handling, EXP, COL, bot
    usefulness where structure and evaluation are separate channels

The Q-box is the recovery mechanism when a single evaluative
slice is required. It does not force the base into a ring signature.

LOCK
    Base division is unchanged.
    Q is derived, optional relative to version 1.1.
    Removing this appendix leaves 1.1 intact.

25. APPENDIX: COLLAPSE AND RECOVERY VIA THE Q-BOX
derived, not a primitive of the base signature
already represented in the architecture
version 1.1 is unchanged if this appendix is absent

This appendix clarifies that RDG does not require an additional homomorphism
    collapse : SID x PED -> R
to relate the calculus to ordinary arithmetic or analysis.
The collapse is already represented internally by the Q-box,
the slice-moderation operator that unifies structure and evaluation
into a single evaluative output.
The recovery map is not missing. It is already encoded
in the relational architecture.
Q is not circ_{S,I}.
Q is not ev.
Q is not q_alpha.
Q does not rewrite a / b.
Q does not identify SID_RDG with PED_MFE inside the base.
RDG is not a conservative extension of a ring.

A. ROLE OF THE Q-BOX

The Q-box is the operator that:
    1. forgets the SID/PED mode separation
    2. projects a relational configuration into a single evaluative slice
    3. recovers the scalar-like behavior of the total fragment
    4. implements the collapse of relational geometry into a
       field-compatible domain

Formally, the Q-box acts as:
    Q tensor SID.S  --->  PED.D

    SID.S    structural channel
    PED.D    dynamic evaluative slice
    the Q-box performs the collapse of relational information
        into a single evaluative output

PED.D is the collapsed slice.
PED.D is not a second name for PED_MFE.
PED.D is not a quotient of U.
ev : SID -> PED stays in PED_MFE and does not certify.
circ_{S,I} : SID x INV -> SID + BOT stays in that coproduct.
Those two maps are not the collapse.

B. COLLAPSE AS A DERIVED OPERATION

Given a structural term X : SID and an evaluative term E : PED,
    collapse(X, E) := Q(X, E)

where Q is the slice-moderation operator.
    Q : SID x PED -> PED.D + BOT

This is not a primitive of the base signature.
It is a derived map implemented by the Q-box.
Thus the recovery map is not missing. It is already encoded
in the relational architecture.

The base equation is unchanged:
    a / b = SID(a) circ_{S,I} iota(PED(b))
Q is applied to the pair (SID(a), PED(b)), not to the composite
as a quotient of U.

Clauses of the same derived map:

    neither factor zero:
        Q(X, E) = s(X, E) : PED.D
        when X is not Z_S and E is not Z_E
        s is the slice interpretation

    denominator zero, expansion:
        Q(X, Z_E) = s_exp(X) : PED.D
        corresponding to EXP(X) = X circ_{S,I} Z_I
        s_exp is the scalar-limit reading
        if s_exp is not declared, the reading is an undefined point,
        not bot

    numerator zero, collapse:
        Q(Z_S, E) = s_col(E) : PED.D
        corresponding to COL(iota(E)) = Z_S circ_{S,I} iota(E)
        s_col is the scalar-limit reading
        if s_col is not declared, the reading is an undefined point,
        not bot

    clash:
        Q(Z_S, Z_E) = bot
        matching 0o_S / 0o_E = bot
        the only clause that returns bot

bot has no Q image. There is no Q(bot).

C. RELATION TO ORDINARY ARITHMETIC

When the SID and PED channels are collapsed by Q:
    the multi-sorted geometry reduces to a single evaluative domain
    division reduces to ordinary division on the total fragment
    singularities EXP, COL, and bot collapse to scalar limits
        or undefined points
    the relational information carried by SID and INV is
        intentionally lost

This is the correct behavior.
RDG is not a conservative extension of a ring.
It is a geometry for simulations that already separate
structure and evaluation.

Scalar-like means the PED.D slice.
A further reading obs : PED.D -> R is not required for the collapse.
No ring homomorphism is claimed beyond this recovery.
EXP(X) != bot and COL(J) != bot remain refusals of version 1.1.
The loss of relational information is the action of Q,
not a rewrite of those locks.
An appendix that sets s_exp or s_col equal to bot is a different appendix.

If Q(X, E) and Q(X', E') both inhabit PED.D, then
    X = X' and E = E' implies Q(X, E) = Q(X', E').
Q does not have to preserve circ_S, circ_E, or circ_{S,I}.
Q forgets the RDG/MFE presentation split in its output only.
Q is not the gate for (a / b) / c ---> product form.
The certificate (U, alpha) is not implied by Q.

D. DESIGN IMPLICATION

RDG's legitimacy does not depend on being arithmetic with a new division.
It depends on:
    internal consistency of the multi-sorted signature
    correctness of the quotient architecture (RDG vs MFE)
    typed cross-mode composition
    explicit singularity handling (EXP, COL, bot)
    usefulness for systems that treat structure and evaluation
        as separate channels

The Q-box provides the recovery mechanism when needed,
without forcing RDG into a ring signature.

LOCK
    The collapse is already the Q-box.
    It is derived, not a base primitive.
    Base division is unchanged.
    Removing this appendix leaves version 1.1 intact.
